{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates lotteries based on the sure outcome (the outcome with minimum absolute value) and then considers the differences between that sure outcome and the other outcomes, weighted by their probabilities. In this case, Lottery A has a 5000 Euro outcome with 0.1% probability and 0 Euros with 99.9% probability. The outcome with the minimum absolute value is 0 Euros (which occurs with 99.9% probability), so this is the 'sure outcome'. The difference between 0 and 5000 is 5000 Euros, which occurs with 0.1% probability. The 'sum of the sure outcome and the differences weighted by their probabilities' would be: 0 * 0.999 + 5000 * 0.001 = 5 Euros. Lottery B has a sure outcome of 5 Euros with 100% probability, so its weighted sum is 5 * 1 = 5 Euros. Both lotteries have the same weighted sum (5 Euros), so the reason would be indifferent between them. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be evaluated using the outcomes and probabilities provided.",
